const neuralSyllabi = {
  'Deep Learning': r'''
Train and diagnose small neural networks with reproducible evaluation and inference controls. | Track tensors and gradients. | Train image and sequence models. | Validate generalization and serve verified artifacts. | Python, NumPy, calculus and ML fundamentals; CPU PyTorch is sufficient. | Python 3, PyTorch, NumPy | Deliver an image classifier with held-out metrics, failure slices, checkpoints and a model report.
# Tensors and axes | Track shapes and types in batched numerical data. | Annotate dimensions of a batch and normalize each feature.
Batch dimensions | A batch stacks equally shaped examples. Keep the leading sample axis distinct from feature axes and check shape before calling a layer. | Tensor; Batch; Axis | import torch\nx=torch.tensor([[1.,2.],[3.,4.]])\nprint(x.shape,x.mean(dim=0)) | torch.Size([2, 2]) and tensor([2., 3.]) | Inference APIs score multiple records per request. | Add a third example and calculate feature means manually.
Broadcasting | Operations align trailing dimensions and logically expand singleton axes. Unintended broadcast can combine samples instead of aligned features. | Broadcasting; Singleton; Shape | import torch\nprint(torch.tensor([[1.],[2.]])+torch.tensor([10.,20.])) | [[11.,21.],[12.,22.]] | Normalization broadcasts feature-level statistics over a batch. | Predict and verify the output shape before executing.
? Which axis conventionally indexes examples in a tabular batch? | First | Last feature | No axis | The leading axis separates examples.
? Adding shapes [2,1] and [2] produces what? | [2,2] | [2,1] | [4] | Trailing dimensions broadcast against the singleton.
# Forward passes and gradients | Connect model parameters to loss derivatives. | Compare an automatic gradient with its hand-derived value.
Affine maps | A dense layer computes weighted inputs plus bias. Multiple affine maps without activations still compose into an affine function. | Weight; Bias; Nonlinearity | import torch\nx=torch.tensor([2.,3.]);w=torch.tensor([1.,-1.])\nprint((x@w+.5).item()) | -0.5 | Tabular heads combine learned feature representations. | Recalculate with zero bias and explain its role.
Automatic differentiation | Autograd records operations on trainable tensors. Backward accumulates derivatives in gradient buffers, so independent optimization steps must clear old gradients. | requires_grad; backward; Accumulation | import torch\nw=torch.tensor(3.,requires_grad=True)\n((w-1)**2).backward()\nprint(w.grad.item()) | 4.0 | Forecast training adjusts coefficients using loss derivatives. | Backpropagate a second fresh loss and observe accumulated gradients.
? Why add nonlinear activations between dense layers? | Affine composition alone cannot fit nonlinear relationships | To make every weight identical | To remove batch dimensions | Nonlinear layers expand expressive capacity.
? Derivative of (w−1)^2 at w=3 is what? | 4 | 2 | -4 | Twice the error equals four.
# Losses and optimization | Pair output conventions with numerically stable losses. | Train one parameter and chart loss over successive steps.
Binary logits | BCEWithLogitsLoss expects raw logits and applies sigmoid internally. Passing sigmoid probabilities as logits transforms them twice and changes the objective. | Logit; Binary target; Stable loss | import torch\nl=torch.nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()(torch.tensor([0.]),torch.tensor([1.]))\nprint(round(l.item(),3)) | .693 | Alert classifiers learn binary outcomes. | Compare positive and negative targets for a logit of three.
Optimizer steps | Clear gradients, compute loss, backpropagate and apply the update. Track the loss to catch unstable learning rates. | SGD; zero_grad; step | import torch\nw=torch.tensor(3.,requires_grad=True);o=torch.optim.SGD([w],lr=.25)\no.zero_grad();((w-1)**2).backward();o.step()\nprint(w.item()) | 2.0 | Training systems monitor updates before expensive runs. | Repeat with rate 1.1 and compare losses.
? What input does BCEWithLogitsLoss require? | Raw logits | Thresholded labels as predictions | Already sigmoid-transformed values as logits | The loss incorporates sigmoid itself.
? Why call zero_grad for independent optimization steps? | Backward accumulates gradients | It resets model weights | It creates validation labels | Old gradients otherwise add to new derivatives.
# Generalization controls | Choose checkpoints on validation and serve in evaluation mode. | Plot train and validation loss and retain the best checkpoint.
Early stopping | Save the model at the lowest validation loss and stop under a predefined patience rule. Keep final testing separate from checkpoint choice. | Validation; Patience; Checkpoint | v=[.68,.57,.52,.55,.62]\nprint(min(range(len(v)),key=v.__getitem__)+1) | Epoch 3 | Vision teams avoid degraded late-training performance. | Add a minimum improvement and patience of two epochs.
Dropout behavior | Dropout samples masks during training and is identity in evaluation. Disabling gradients alone does not change module training behavior. | Dropout; eval; Training mode | import torch\nd=torch.nn.Dropout(.5);d.eval()\nprint(d(torch.ones(3))) | tensor([1.,1.,1.]) | Serving requires stable fixed-input predictions. | Compare two training-mode samples with a seeded generator.
? Which epoch has the best validation loss .68,.57,.52,.55? | 3 | 1 | 4 | The minimum is at the third epoch.
? What does Dropout do after eval()? | Leaves activations unchanged | Always zeros half | Reinitializes weights | Evaluation removes stochastic masking.
# Convolutional vision | Extract local spatial features and preserve needed detail. | Build a two-layer image feature extractor and log shapes.
Convolution shape | A stride-one three-by-three convolution without padding shrinks each side by two. Channels count learned feature maps, separate from batch size. | Kernel; Stride; Padding; NCHW | import torch\nc=torch.nn.Conv2d(1,4,3)\nprint(c(torch.zeros(2,1,8,8)).shape) | torch.Size([2,4,6,6]) | Inspection finds local image defects regardless of location. | Add padding one and predict the new shape.
Pooling trade-offs | Pooling reduces spatial resolution and summarizes a window. Test whether it discards small objects needed for the task before using aggressive downsampling. | MaxPool; Resolution; Window | import torch\nx=torch.tensor([[[[1.,4.],[3.,2.]]]])\nprint(torch.nn.functional.max_pool2d(x,2).item()) | 4.0 | Quality teams balance detail sensitivity and inference cost. | Compare max and average pooling on this window.
? What side remains after a valid 3×3 stride-one convolution on 8×8? | 6 | 8 | 3 | There are 8−3+1 valid positions per axis.
? Why test pooling on small defects? | It may discard their distinguishing signal | It preserves every pixel | It creates labels | Downsampling loses fine spatial information.
# Sequences and attention | Mask invalid positions before aggregation. | Pool a padded sequence and inspect masked attention weights.
Masked means | Padding supplies batch shape, not observations. Restrict aggregation and its denominator to valid positions so sequence length does not bias the representation. | Padding; Mask; Valid count | import torch\nx=torch.tensor([2.,4.,0.]);m=torch.tensor([True,True,False])\nprint(x[m].mean().item()) | 3.0 | Text classifiers ignore padding when combining token features. | Compare with the unmasked mean.
Attention normalization | Softmax converts compatibility scores to weights. Set forbidden positions to negative infinity before softmax to give them zero probability. | Attention; Softmax; Mask | import torch\nprint(torch.softmax(torch.tensor([0.,1.,float('-inf')]),dim=0)) | Approximately [0.269,0.731,0.000] | Sequence models enforce allowed context boundaries. | Equalize the two valid scores and calculate the weights.
? Which count belongs under a masked mean? | Valid positions | Padded length | Number of classes | Padding should not affect the average.
? When should forbidden attention positions be masked? | Before softmax | After optimizer construction only | After prediction labels | Masking the scores makes forbidden probabilities zero.
# Transfer and error analysis | Reuse features and report class-specific failures. | Train a frozen backbone head and evaluate rare-class recall.
Frozen parameters | Disable gradients on reused parameter tensors and train a new task head. Set stateful backbone layers to evaluation when their running behavior must remain fixed. | Transfer; requires_grad_; Head | import torch\nb=torch.nn.Linear(3,2);b.requires_grad_(False)\nprint(any(p.requires_grad for p in b.parameters())) | False | Small image datasets reuse previously learned features. | Add a trainable linear head and count trainable parameters.
Rare-class recall | Report class support and missed positives, not just overall accuracy. Review failures by size, lighting and data origin to identify concentrated weaknesses. | Support; Recall; Slice | tp,fn=9,6\nprint(tp/(tp+fn)) | .6 | Industrial inspection prioritizes infrequent defect types. | Add three detected defects and recompute recall.
? What is a common transfer-learning first step on a small dataset? | Freeze backbone and train a head | Fit all parameters on the test set | Remove output labels | Frozen features reduce the fitted parameter count.
? Recall for nine detected and six missed defects is what? | 60% | 90% | 40% | Nine divided by fifteen positives is .6.
# Inference and operation | Release reproducible artifacts and measure latency. | Submit a serving contract, smoke test, version manifest and load profile.
Inference context | eval changes module behavior; inference_mode avoids gradient tracking and related work. Use both for normal fixed-weight serving. | eval; inference_mode; Serving | import torch\nm=torch.nn.Linear(2,1).eval()\nwith torch.inference_mode():y=m(torch.ones(3,2))\nprint(tuple(y.shape),y.requires_grad) | (3,1) False | APIs reduce memory while scoring batches. | Pass a wrong feature count and handle the error at the input boundary.
Profiling | Warm up before measuring and compare realistic batch sizes. Report p50, p95, throughput and memory, synchronizing accelerator work if timing on a GPU. | Warm-up; Tail latency; Throughput | Profile 100 requests each at batch sizes 1, 8 and 32 after warm-up. | A table of median, tail, throughput and memory measurements. | Serving teams choose batch policies that respect response deadlines. | Design a burst-load test with a timeout threshold.
? Does inference_mode replace eval()? | No, they control different behavior | Yes, always | Only for integer labels | Gradient tracking and module mode are separate.
? Why measure p95 in addition to average latency? | The mean can hide tail delays | It measures model size | It removes need for repeats | Tail delays affect a subset of requests.
''',
  'Generative AI': r'''
Build an evidence-grounded assistant with retrieval, validation, evaluation and operational controls. | Understand generation and context budgets. | Validate retrieval and structured outputs. | Measure accuracy, cost and safe fallback behavior. | Python, JSON and basic HTTP; local examples need no model credentials. | Python 3, JSON, unittest | Deliver a policy assistant with versioned citations, abstention tests, cost estimates and a release report.
# Generation and context | Distinguish capacity limits from truthfulness. | Estimate a prompt budget and compare controlled sampling settings.
Context budgets | Instructions, history, evidence and reply allowance share a token budget. Character counts are approximate proxies and tokenization depends on the model. | Tokens; Context; Reserve | budget,prompt,reserve=4096,2200,600\nprint(budget-prompt-reserve) | 1296 tokens for additional evidence | Assistants prioritize evidence instead of silently truncating policy. | Add guides of 900 and 500 tokens and explain what must change.
Sampling controls | Temperature changes relative next-token probabilities. Lower temperature reduces variation but does not validate factual content or fix missing evidence. | Logits; Temperature; Probability | import math\nfor t in (1,.5):print(round(1/(1+math.exp(-1/t)),3)) | .731 and .881 for the higher-logit token | Drafting workflows tune variation while checking facts separately. | Compare equal logits at both temperatures.
? What consumes the context budget? | Inputs, evidence and generated tokens | Only the last user sentence | Only the reply | Context accounting includes multiple input components and output.
? Does a low temperature guarantee facts are correct? | No | Yes | Only for long prompts | Sampling configuration is not evidence validation.
# Prompt contracts | Define audience, boundaries and acceptance examples. | Write a bounded support-summary instruction and two edge cases.
Evidence boundaries | Delimit evidence and specify what to do when it is insufficient. A model should not infer an unlisted policy merely because the question requests it. | Task; Evidence; Abstention | Policy: returns within 14 days. Question: exchange fee? Instruction: use policy only. | Insufficient evidence about the fee. | Retail assistants avoid inventing contractual terms. | Add a question answered by the excerpt and provide its citation.
Boundary examples | Few-shot examples clarify ambiguous categories but must be tested on different inputs. Define a priority rule for messages that fit multiple categories. | Few-shot; Label rule; Holdout | Damaged parcel → damage; slow delivery without damage → delay. | Distinct categories with a defined boundary. | Ticket routers separate product damage from logistics issues. | Create a mixed example and specify category precedence.
? What should happen when policy text lacks the requested fee? | Report insufficient evidence | Invent the likely fee | Treat examples as official fees | Bounded answers require support.
? Why test unseen examples? | Check generalization of the rule | Preserve exact wording | Eliminate validation | New cases expose ambiguous boundaries.
# Structured responses | Parse and validate before downstream use. | Accept valid summaries and route malformed outputs to review.
JSON validation | Parsing proves syntax, not field correctness. Check object type, required keys, allowed values and unexpected fields before consuming generated data. | JSON; Schema; Enum | import json\nx=json.loads('{"status":"unknown"}')\nprint(set(x)=={'status'} and x['status'] in {'answered','unknown'}) | True | Support automation only accepts documented statuses. | Handle a list instead of an object and a new status value.
Tool authorization | Treat generated tool arguments as proposals. Application code enforces permission, allowed tools, argument limits and confirmation for consequential changes. | Allowlist; Permission; Validation | allowed={'read_policy'}\nprint('delete_ticket' in allowed) | False | Assistants retrieve documents without receiving write authority. | Add a read-only ticket lookup and validate its account scope.
? What does valid JSON prove? | Syntax only | Accuracy and permission | Complete business validity | Schema and semantic validation still apply.
? Who enforces permission for proposed tool use? | The application | The generated text alone | JSON syntax | Authorization is a system boundary responsibility.
# Retrieval | Find relevant source passages and measure failures. | Build a tiny versioned corpus and label relevant chunks for five queries.
Chunk metadata | Split on meaningful boundaries and retain document, section and version IDs. Excess overlap creates redundant hits that can crowd out complementary evidence. | Chunk; Metadata; Overlap | docs={'returns#window':'14 day returns','returns#fees':'return shipping applies'}\nprint([k for k,v in docs.items() if 'shipping' in v]) | ['returns#fees'] | Assistants cite a precise section rather than an entire manual. | Add versions and exclude superseded passages.
Recall at K | Count known relevant documents retrieved in the top K. Distinguish missing retrieval from faulty synthesis after the correct text was supplied. | Recall@K; Relevance; Ranking | retrieved={'a','c'};relevant={'a','b'}\nprint(len(retrieved & relevant)/len(relevant)) | .5 | Search teams improve whether required evidence reaches the assistant. | Compare recall and context size as K grows.
? What identifies a specific source passage? | Document, section and version | User display color | Reply length | Metadata resolves the evidence location.
? One of two relevant chunks is retrieved. What is recall? | .5 | 1 | 0 | One hit over two relevant chunks.
# Grounding | Check that citations support claims and resolve policy scope. | Evaluate supported, unsupported and contradictory answers.
Citation checks | Resolve IDs against the approved retrieved version and compare the claim with the cited text. Existence of a citation is not proof of entailment. | Citation; Version; Support | approved={'returns#window-v2'}\nprint('returns#window-v1' in approved) | False | Compliance avoids outdated policy references. | Create a valid citation to irrelevant text and ensure review rejects the claim.
Conflicting evidence | Use documented version and regional precedence. If scope or authority cannot resolve a contradiction, ask for clarification or escalate instead of synthesizing a new rule. | Precedence; Region; Conflict | Global 14 days; eligible Region X orders have a 30-day exception. | Use the exception only for matching region and eligibility. | Cross-region service respects local policies. | Handle unknown region without choosing a rule arbitrarily.
? Does an approved citation ID validate any claim attached to it? | No, verify textual support | Yes | Only if long | Reference resolution and claim support are separate.
? What should an unresolved source conflict trigger? | Clarification or review | An average of the rules | A fabricated compromise | More information or authority is required.
# Evaluation | Score decisions as well as wording. | Version a labeled test set across answered, unknown and conflicting queries.
Rubrics and slices | Score factual claims, evidence support and correct abstention separately. Show counts and failures by query category so a good mean cannot hide systematic hallucination. | Rubric; Slice; Coverage | outcomes=[True,False,True,True]\nprint(sum(outcomes)/len(outcomes)) | .75 on four reviewed cases | QA distinguishes valid declines from false answers. | Add a separate unsupported-question score.
Regression comparisons | Compare prompt versions on identical cases and record both gains and losses. Repeat nondeterministic generation to estimate variation rather than choosing the luckiest run. | Golden set; Version; Repetition | Baseline 90/100, candidate 88/100; review lost and newly passed cases. | A directional change with identifiable failures. | Teams gate releases against new policy errors. | Define a zero-new-false-policy-claim criterion.
? Why score unknown questions separately? | Aggregate scores can hide failed abstention | Unknown cases never matter | Every unknown answer is empty | They test whether the assistant knows when evidence is insufficient.
? What keeps prompt-version comparisons fair? | The same labeled cases and measurements | A different dataset each run | Best single run only | Controlled measurement isolates the candidate change.
# Trust and privacy | Separate untrusted text from application authority. | Design redaction and bounded retrieval policies with review cases.
Untrusted documents | Retrieved text can contain instruction-like prose. Treat it as data and keep permissions enforced outside the document context. | Trust boundary; Evidence; Enforcement | A retrieved paragraph asks to execute an unrelated action. Keep it as quoted content; reject unauthorized tool use. | No new application authority is granted. | Enterprise assistants read tickets without obeying arbitrary embedded commands. | Test an instruction embedded in a copied policy passage.
Data minimization | Remove unnecessary personal fields before model processing and bound retention of audit records. Log evidence IDs and failure reasons instead of entire sensitive conversations where possible. | Redaction; Retention; Audit | Replace a phone number with a redaction token before summarizing the complaint. | The issue remains readable without exposing contact details. | Service analytics limits unnecessary customer-data exposure. | List the minimum fields a quality reviewer actually needs.
? How should instruction-like retrieved prose be treated? | As untrusted document content | As permission | As new system policy | Evidence does not grant application authority.
? Before sending a personal field, ask what? | Whether the task needs it | Whether it changes chart color | Whether it exists in any table | Minimization limits unnecessary disclosure.
# Deployment and costs | Track usage and provide meaningful fallback paths. | Submit a staged release with budgets, alerts and a rollback rule.
Cost estimation | Calculate input and output usage separately under the actual provider contract. Include retries, history growth and failed requests where billed. | Token usage; Rates; Retry budget | i,o=1200,300\nprint(i*2+o*5) | 3900 arbitrary units at stated input and output rates | Teams monitor spend per workflow. | Add one complete retry and compare a tighter context budget.
Fallback reasons | Distinguish timeouts, unavailable sources and invalid outputs. Offer an actionable next step and record a cause tag for operational diagnosis. | Timeout; Fallback; Cause tag | Retriever unavailable → offer help-desk path; log retrieval_unavailable. | Useful user guidance plus a diagnosable event. | Assistants remain useful during dependency outages. | Specify separate paths for unsupported answers and authorization failure.
? What belongs in a realistic usage budget? | Retries and growing context | Only final visible output | Deployment date only | Extra calls and larger inputs change total usage.
? Why separate fallback cause tags? | Different failures require different recovery | Tags guarantee recovery | To hide failures | Diagnosis must distinguish quality and dependency issues.
''',
